# H1 Associate final exam, 42 questions across m01 to m06.
# Sources: digest sections 1 to 12 and 28. Every figure is printed there.

q(1, "UGHELLI's 4 DART cases in 2318640 hours are wanted for an IOGP comparison, per 1,000,000 hours. What figure is right?",
 "1.725149, which is 4 x 1,000,000 / 2318640 and five times the OSHA base DART rate.",
 ["0.345030, the DART rate on the 200,000 hour base, which can go straight into a per million comparison.",
  "3.881586, since IOGP compares recordables and the DART count should be widened to all 9 cases first.",
  "0.862575, the DART count on the million base."],
 "4 x 1,000,000 / 2318640 is 1.725149, which is 5.000000 times 0.345030. Putting 0.345030 into a per million column understates the site five times over. 3.881586 uses the 9 recordables and 0.862575 the 2 lost time cases, so each rates a different class from the one asked for."),

q(3, "UGHELLI's 4 Tier 2 process safety events are rated per 1,000,000 hours of its 2318640. What does the engine return?",
 "1.725149, from 4 x 1,000,000 / 2318640, on one of the two bases a PSE rate accepts.",
 ["0.431287, since a Tier 2 event is rated like a Tier 1 event and the tier label changes only the formula line.",
  "0.345030, the same count per 200,000 hours.",
  "5.454545, since Tier 2 rates are taken from the golden and apply to every site."],
 "Four events times a million over 2318640 hours gives 1.725149, and the million hour base is one of the two a PSE rate may use. What sets the answer is the count the tier carries: UGHELLI's one Tier 1 event gives 0.431287 on this base. The golden's 5.454545 comes from a different workplace, and a figure on the smaller base cannot be moved across bases unchanged."),

q(0, "The vendored golden checks nine Tier 2 events against 1650000 hours per million. What does the engine return, and does it agree?",
 "5.454545, with a relative difference of 0.",
 ["0.242424, which is the rate the golden records for this case on the million hour base.",
  "A refusal naming `tier`, since the engine rates only Tier 1 events against the golden and holds every Tier 2 case back.",
  "1.725149 per 1,000,000 hours, since every Tier 2 rate on the million base reads the same whatever the hours."],
 "9 x 1,000,000 / 1650000 is 5.454545, and the golden rate is the same. 0.242424 is the other golden case, 2 Tier 1 events in the same hours on the 200,000 base. Tiers 1 and 2 are both accepted, and the engine divides once on the base named."),

q(2, "Two Tier 1 events are logged over 1650000 hours in a second golden case, and the caller names the 200,000 base. What does the engine print?",
 "0.242424 per 200,000 hours, from 2 x 200,000 / 1650000, matching the golden.",
 ["0.086257, which is the Tier 1 rate for any site with a single Tier 1 event recorded in a year.",
  "5.454545, since Tier 1 weighs more.",
  "A refusal naming `base`."],
 "2 x 200,000 / 1650000 is 0.242424. 0.086257 is UGHELLI's single event over its own 2318640 hours, a different workplace. The engine weights no tier; the tier is an input that names which count is being rated. Both the 200,000 and 1,000,000 bases are accepted for a PSE rate."),

q(2, "The golden case for the severity rate puts 312 days lost over 400000 hours on the 200,000 base. What does the engine return?",
 "156.000000, which is 312 x 200,000 / 400000: days lost times the base over the hours.",
 ["8.280716, the severity rate the engine prints on the OSHA base for every site in the golden set of cases.",
  "24.863388, since 312 days must be converted to the IOGP base before a severity rate is computed.",
  "3.500000, since the 400000 hours are the BLS example's and give the same rate."],
 "312 x 200,000 / 400000 is 156.000000, which the golden records with a relative difference of 0. 8.280716 is UGHELLI's own severity rate from 96 days, and 24.863388 the second golden case of 45.5 days on the million base. 3.500000 is a recordable rate from a count of 7 over the same hours."),

q(1, "Looked at alone, the compression station at KWALE logged 3 recordables across 512300 hours. What does that one site read?",
 "1.171189: its 3 cases over its own 512300 hours, on the 200,000 base.",
 ["0.968312, since every site in a field reads the field's pooled rate once the counts are summed.",
  "1.754760, the mean of the three site rates, which is what each site's own rate is measured against.",
  "0.840001, the rate KWALE's flow station reads over its own hours."],
 "Each site rate is its own count over its own hours: 3 x 200,000 / 512300 is 1.171189. 0.968312 is the whole field's pooled rate, 1.754760 the unweighted mean of the three site rates, and 0.840001 the flow station's rate from 8 recordables in 1904760 hours."),

q(0, "KWALE's jetty reads 3.253090 per 200,000 hours, the highest site rate on the field. What is behind that figure?",
 "A single recordable in only 61480 hours.",
 ["The jetty had the most recordables of the three sites, and its rate follows its count.",
  "The jetty's rate is computed on the IOGP base while the other two are on the OSHA base, so it looks larger than theirs.",
  "The jetty's hours are counted twice, once for the site and once again for the pooled total of the field."],
 "1 x 200,000 / 61480 is 3.253090. One event over very few hours makes a large rate, which is why the jetty can pull an unweighted mean of rates up so far. The flow station had the most recordables, 8, and all three site rates are on the same base."),

q(3, "The jetty worked 61480 of KWALE's 2478540 hours. What fraction is that, and in which of the two field figures does it act as the jetty's weight?",
 "0.024805, and it is the jetty's weight in the pooled rate of 0.968312.",
 ["0.333333, one site of three, and it is the jetty's weight in the pooled rate.",
  "0.024805, and it is the jetty's weight in the unweighted average of the site rates.",
  "3.253090, its own rate, which sets its weight."],
 "Dividing 61480 by 2478540 gives 0.024805. Sum then divide lets each site count in proportion to the hours it worked, so that fraction is the jetty's say in 0.968312. An unweighted average of the three site rates hands every site 0.333333 whatever it worked, which is why the jetty's 3.253090 lifts that average so far."),

q(0, "Month 4 of the AKASO stream holds 3 recordables over 205880 hours. Taken on its own, what does that month read?",
 "2.914319, from 3 x 200,000 / 205880, the month's own count over its own hours.",
 ["1.883239, the first month's rate, since every month in a rolling series reads the rate of the series' opening month.",
  "1.208038, the rate of the window holding month 4.",
  "10.964912, as the worst month of the year."],
 "3 x 200,000 / 205880 is 2.914319. A month has its own rate, and the rolling rate of 1.208038 is the pooled figure over twelve months. 10.964912 belongs to month 11, whose single recordable fell in only 18240 hours; the largest monthly rate comes from the fewest hours here."),

q(1, "AKASO's window 2 covers months 2 to 13: 13 recordables in 2149730 hours. What rolling rate does the engine print?",
 "1.209454: sum the window's 13 cases and its 2149730 hours, then divide once on the base.",
 ["2.028541, the mean of the monthly rates in the window, since the engine reports that figure as the window's rate.",
  "1.208038, since the rolling rate stays fixed until the count in the window changes.",
  "1.207931, the rate of the window running from month 3 to month 14."],
 "13 x 200,000 / 2149730 is 1.209454. The count matches window 1 but the hours differ, so the rate moves slightly; a rate follows hours as well as counts. 2.028541 is window 2's mean of monthly rates, printed beside it for comparison only, and 1.207931 is window 3."),

q(2, "AKASO's window 3 prints 1.207931 and, beside it, 2.027310. Which one is the rolling rate, and what is the other?",
 "1.207931 is the rolling rate; 2.027310 is the mean of the monthly rates.",
 ["2.027310 is the rolling rate, and 1.207931 is the same window with the short month taken out of it.",
  "1.207931 is the rolling rate on the OSHA base, and 2.027310 is the same rate restated on a larger base.",
  "Both are rolling rates, one per half of the window."],
 "The rolling rate is the window's summed recordables over its summed hours, 1.207931. The engine prints the mean of the monthly rates, 2.027310, for comparison only; it sits higher because the short month's rate enters with the weight of a full one. Both are on the 200,000 base and neither drops a month."),

q(3, "IOGP's 2022 FAR was 1.279566, from 33 fatalities in 2579000000 hours, the highest of the five years. What does this tier say about reading one high year?",
 "Much of the movement between years is what a small count does; a five year window pools more events and moves less.",
 ["It shows the industry became less safe in 2022, since the rate rose above every other year in the window.",
  "It should be removed from the five year figure as an outlier before the window is pooled.",
  "It is a data error, since the engine refuses a FAR above one."],
 "The yearly rates run from 0.550271 in 2020 to 1.279566 in 2022, and a single year rests on a few dozen fatalities, so much of that swing is small count noise. A five year window pools more events and hours. Nothing in this tier removes a year from a window, and the engine refuses no FAR for its size."),

q(3, "IOGP's five year FAR by sum then divide, 0.826095, sits a little below the mean of its yearly FARs, 0.833228. What pulls it down?",
 "Hour weighting: 2022's high rate of 1.279566 sits on fewer hours than an average year, and 2024's lower 0.769438 on the most.",
 ["The pooled figure leaves out 2022, whose rate of 1.279566 was the highest of the five years.",
  "The mean of rates is computed on a larger base, which lifts it by a small fixed factor.",
  "Both are rounded to two decimals first, and the rounding lowers the pooled figure."],
 "The mean gives each year a fifth; the pooled figure weights each year by its hours, which run from 2544201000 in 2020 to 4158877000 in 2024. 2022, the highest rate, worked fewer hours than an average year and so counts for less than a fifth, while 2024, below the mean, worked the most and counts for more. Together they pull the pooled figure a little under the mean of rates. Every year is in both figures, both are on the 100,000,000 base, and the engine rounds nothing."),

q(0, "A caller passes a negative number of days to `severityRate`. What does the engine say?",
 "A refusal naming `daysLost`: days lost must be a finite number of days, zero or more.",
 ["A refusal naming `count`, with the message that an event count cannot be a fraction or below zero.",
  "A severity rate of 0.000000, since negative days are read as no days lost at all.",
  "A refusal naming `base`, since days lost are checked only after a base is named in the call."],
 "The engine's own words are: daysLost must be a finite number of days, zero or more. The severity rate takes days, which may be fractional but may not be negative, and the field it names is `daysLost`. The count message belongs to `incidenceRate`, and the engine never turns a bad input into zero."),

q(1, "A caller asks for a PSE rate and leaves the tier out. What comes back?",
 "No rate; the engine refuses and names the `tier` field.",
 ["A rate on the assumption the events are Tier 1, since the more serious tier is the safer default for a report.",
  "A rate with the tier set to null in the formula line, which reads PSE count x base / total work hours.",
  "A refusal naming `pseCount`, since a count without a tier cannot be matched to any class of event."],
 "The engine's own words are: tier must be 1 or 2: classify the events against API RP 754 before rating them. The same message answers tier 3. The engine never picks a tier for the caller, because classifying an event needs the licensed threshold tables it does not hold."),

q(2, "`fatalAccidentRate` is called with 32 fatalities and 0 hours. Its base is fixed. Does the fixed base change what happens?",
 "No: with no hours there is no FAR, fixed base or not, and `exposureHours` is the field refused.",
 ["Yes: the fixed base lets the engine fall back on IOGP's 2024 hours of 4158877000.",
  "Yes: a FAR with no hours returns 0.000000.",
  "No, but the field named is `fatalities`."],
 "The engine's own words: exposureHours must be a finite number of hours above zero: a rate over no exposure is undefined. A fixed base does not mean the FAR accepts anything; with no hours there is no rate. The engine carries no benchmark hours and never invents a zero."),

q(1, "A count of minus 1 recordable reaches `incidenceRate`. Which message comes back?",
 "The same one a fractional count gets: a count must be a whole number, zero or more.",
 ["A message that negative counts are corrected to zero, followed by a rate of 0.000000 on the base named.",
  "A refusal naming `exposureHours`, as a mistyped box.",
  "A rate on the absolute value, 1 recordable."],
 "The engine's own words for both a fractional and a negative count: count must be a whole number, zero or more: an event count is not a fraction. The field named is `count`. The engine corrects nothing and never guesses which box was mistyped."),

q(0, "`severityRate` is called with UGHELLI's 96 days and 2318640 hours and no base. What happens?",
 "It is refused with the same base message `incidenceRate` gives: there is no default.",
 ["It returns 41.403581, since the severity rate defaults to the ANSI Z16.1 base of 1,000,000 hours.",
  "It returns 8.280716 on the OSHA base, the base the severity rate always uses.",
  "It returns 48.000000 days a case, since without a base the engine divides by the cases."],
 "The severity rate has two common bases, the OSHA style 200,000 and the ANSI Z16.1 convention's 1,000,000, so the engine will not guess. A missing base draws exactly this message from `incidenceRate` too. Days per case is not a figure the engine computes on any call."),

q(2, "A reader wants UGHELLI's days lost per lost time case from the engine. Which function returns it?",
 "None: it is derived by hand, 96 over 2.",
 ["`severityRate`, which returns 48.000000 when the base is set to the number of lost time cases.",
  "`incidenceRate`, called with the days as the count and the lost time cases passed in as the hours.",
  "`pooledRate`, whose meanOfPeriodRates returns the average days for each lost time case in the list."],
 "The engine does not compute days per case. Its one function in this area, `severityRate`, returns days lost times a base over hours, 8.280716 per 200,000 hours for UGHELLI. Days per case, 96 over 2, is 48.000000 and has no hours in it. Passing cases where hours belong would produce a number with no meaning."),

q(3, "A report says injuries became less serious this year because days lost per case fell. What could still be true of the site's severity rate?",
 "It could have risen, because more injuries each slightly shorter can raise total days per block of hours.",
 ["It must have fallen too, since both measures are built from the same days lost and move together.",
  "It must be unchanged, since the severity rate counts cases and the days per case counts days.",
  "Nothing, since the engine refuses a severity rate once days per case is known."],
 "Days per case divides by the number of cases; the severity rate divides by hours. A site with more injuries, each a little shorter, can show falling days per case and a rising severity rate at once. Both readings are arithmetic on real records, which is why any figure for how serious injuries were must travel with its denominator."),

q(0, "In one incident, 3 workers die. How many does the incident add to the count behind the FAR, and to the count behind the fatal incident rate?",
 "3 to the FAR count and 1 to the fatal incident count.",
 ["1 to each count, since both rates count events, and one incident is one event on the record however many died.",
  "3 to each count, as both rates count deaths.",
  "3 to the FAR count and none to the other."],
 "The FAR counts people who died; the fatal incident rate counts incidents that killed someone. IOGP's 2024 figures show the difference: 32 fatalities in 21 fatal incidents give 0.769438 and 0.504944 on the same hours and the same base. Counting the incident once in the FAR loses two of the deaths."),

q(2, "Why does the FAR use a base as large as 100,000,000 hours?",
 "Fatalities are rare, and a base that size turns the rate into a number near one that a reader can compare.",
 ["IOGP's members work about that many hours a year, so the base describes one year of the whole industry.",
  "It is the product of the OSHA and IOGP bases, which keeps the FAR consistent with both conventions.",
  "A small base would round the FAR to zero in the engine."],
 "The 2024 figure rests on 32 fatalities over more than four billion hours; on the 200,000 base that rate would be well under one hundredth, awkward to read and easy to misprint. A base of 100,000,000 gives 0.769438. IOGP's yearly hours run to billions and have nothing to do with the choice of base, and the engine prints six decimals on any base."),

q(1, "A PSE rate report must sit beside a company's recordable rate, which is published per 200,000 hours. Which base should the PSE rate use?",
 "200,000, to match the injury rate.",
 ["100,000,000, since process safety events are rare and rated like fatalities on the largest base the engine has.",
  "1,000,000, since API RP 754 fixes the million hour base for every PSE rate whatever the injury rate uses.",
  "Any base at all, since the engine accepts any positive number of hours as a base for a PSE rate."],
 "The engine takes the PSE rate consistent with the basis for calculating the Company's occupational injury rate, so a company reporting per 200,000 hours reports its PSE rate the same way. Only 200,000 and 1,000,000 are accepted, and the FAR base is refused."),

q(3, "Which exported `RATE_BASES` name should a caller pass to rate UGHELLI's recordables for an IOGP pack, and what label comes back?",
 "`IOGP_1M`, 1000000, labelled per 1,000,000 hours (IOGP).",
 ["`OSHA_200K`, since IOGP converts every OSHA figure itself, labelled per 200,000 hours.",
  "`FAR_100M`, labelled per 100,000,000 hours (FAR, IOGP), since IOGP's name is on that label.",
  "None: the engine picks it."],
 "The exported names are `OSHA_200K` 200000, `IOGP_1M` 1000000 and `FAR_100M` 100000000. On `IOGP_1M` UGHELLI reads 3.881586 with the label per 1,000,000 hours (IOGP). IOGP's name appearing on the FAR label does not make it the base for recordables, and the engine picks no base itself."),

q(1, "A contractor's OSHA DART rate of 0.345030 is pasted into a column of IOGP per million DART rates. What happens to the contractor's standing, and what should the entry read?",
 "It looks five times better than it is; the entry should read 1.725149.",
 ["It looks five times worse than it is, since per million figures run smaller; the entry should stay as it is.",
  "Nothing changes, as a DART rate needs no conversion.",
  "It looks slightly better; it should read 0.862575."],
 "The same 4 DART cases in 2318640 hours read 0.345030 per 200,000 and 1.725149 per 1,000,000. Beside per million figures, the smaller number looks five times better than the record. Dividing by five goes the wrong direction, and 0.862575 is the lost time rate on the IOGP base."),

q(3, "A site's lost time rate reads above its DART rate on the same hours and base. What does that tell you?",
 "The counting is inconsistent somewhere: on one set of definitions lost time cases are a subset of DART cases, so they cannot outnumber them.",
 ["The site had a bad year for lost time and a good year for restricted work.",
  "The two rates were computed on different bases and cannot be compared.",
  "Nothing, since the engine never checks the order of classes."],
 "On a site that classifies consistently every lost time case is also a DART case, so with shared hours and base the lost time rate cannot exceed the DART rate; UGHELLI's 2 inside 4 give 0.172515 and 0.345030. Mixing definitions is one way to break the order: IOGP's lost time count takes in fatalities, so beside a DART count drawn up another way it can run higher. The premise already fixes the base. It is true that the engine checks no order, which is exactly why the reader must."),

q(0, "With KWALE's mothballed fourth site listed, the engine's mean of rates still reads 1.754760. Over how many sites is that mean taken?",
 "Three, the sites with hours.",
 ["Four, with the mothballed site's rate entered as 0.000000, which is why the figure matches the three site mean exactly.",
  "Four, with the mothballed site given the pooled rate of 0.968312 so that it cannot pull the mean in either direction.",
  "Two, since the jetty is also set aside for having too few hours to give a trustworthy rate."],
 "The mean is taken over periods with hours; the mothballed site's rate is null and it is counted in `periodsWithoutHours`. Entered as zero it would pull the mean below 1.754760. The engine never fills a null with another site's figure, and the jetty is kept with its 61480 hours."),

q(2, "In a rolling call, the first window covers two periods with no hours. Does the engine list that window, and what does it say?",
 "Yes, with a null rate and the reason: no hours in this window: the rate is undefined.",
 ["No, empty windows are dropped, so the list simply starts at the first window that has any hours in it.",
  "Yes, with a rate of 0.000000, since no events happened in it and an empty window is a clean one.",
  "No, the whole call is refused, naming `exposureHours` for the empty periods."],
 "The window is still listed, with a reason, so a reader sees that a window existed and had nothing in it. A zero would claim that people worked and nothing happened. A whole call is refused only when a period has events and no hours, or when the hours sum to zero."),

q(0, "The engine has one function for TRIR, DART rate and lost time rate. Why only one?",
 "The arithmetic is the same for every class; only the count changes, and the caller classifies it.",
 ["It detects the class from the size of the count and applies the matching definition by itself.",
  "Only recordables are rated; DART and lost time rates are derived from the TRIR by a fixed fraction.",
  "Two separate functions would disagree about the base, which is why they were merged into one."],
 "Every class is count x base / exposureHours, so `incidenceRate` serves all three with the caller's count. The engine never sees the word DART and cannot tell what a count of 4 is. UGHELLI's DART rate comes from its 4 DART cases, and no fixed fraction of its TRIR is involved."),

q(1, "In AKASO's window 1, how does the short month 11 enter the rolling rate?",
 "As 1 recordable added to the count and 18240 hours added to the hours, so its one event counts like any other in the window.",
 ["As its own rate of 10.964912, entered once with the weight of a full month of work beside the others.",
  "It is left out, since a month with so few hours is treated like the shutdown month.",
  "As 1 recordable, with its hours rounded up to a full month."],
 "In the rolling rate the short month counts for exactly the hours it holds: 13 recordables over 2152250 hours give 1.208038. Its own rate of 10.964912 enters only the mean of the monthly rates, where it pulls that figure up to 2.026485. Only the shutdown month, with no hours, has no rate."),

q(3, "When a twelve month window moves on by one month, what can change the rolling rate?",
 "Only the month leaving and the month joining.",
 ["Every month in the window, since the engine recomputes each month's weight whenever the window moves on by one.",
  "Only the newest month, since the oldest month stays in the window until the end of the year it belongs to.",
  "Nothing, since a rolling rate is fixed once a full year has been counted."],
 "One month drops out of the back and one comes in at the front, and the rate changes only by what those two months differ by. That is why AKASO's first three windows read 1.208038, 1.209454 and 1.207931, and why window 4 rises to 1.306019 when a month with 1 recordable replaces one with none."),

q(2, "UGHELLI's severity rate is 8.280716 per 200,000 hours. What can that single figure tell you about how its days were lost?",
 "Not whether they came from a few long absences or many short ones; the rate rises with either.",
 ["That its injuries were mostly long absences, since a severity rate above the lost time rate means long cases.",
  "That each injury kept a worker away for about 8 days, since the rate is days for each case on the base.",
  "That time charges were added for its most serious case."],
 "A severity rate says how many days were lost for each block of hours worked; it rises when injuries are more serious or more frequent and cannot say which. It is not a per case figure, which for UGHELLI would be 48.000000 days. The engine adds no time charges."),

q(2, "The pooled rate's note reads: meanOfPeriodRates is the unweighted mean of the per-period rates over periods with hours; it is NOT the pooled rate. What is the note guarding against?",
 "A reader taking the mean of site rates, printed beside the pooled rate, for the rate of the whole workforce.",
 ["A reader using the pooled rate for a single site, which the note says must be taken from the mean instead.",
  "A reader assuming the pooled rate leaves out the periods with no hours, which the note says it counts as zeros.",
  "A reader rounding the pooled rate."],
 "The engine returns both figures, labelled, so both can be seen; the note says which one is the pooled rate. On KWALE the mean is 1.754760 and the pooled rate 0.968312. Periods with no hours add nothing to the pooled sums and are left out of the mean, never counted as zeros."),

q(0, "Feed the fatalities and hours for 2020 to 2024 into the pooling function, naming the FAR base. Which two figures does the result carry?",
 "0.826095, with 0.833228 as the mean of the five yearly rates beside it.",
 ["0.833228 as the rate, with 0.826095 beside it as the figure IOGP publishes for its members.",
  "0.769438, the latest year's rate, with the other years reported only in the list of period rates.",
  "0.826095, with 1.279566 beside it as the highest year."],
 "The pooled rate sums 14, 20, 33, 27 and 32 fatalities over the five years' hours and divides once: 0.826095. The companion is the unweighted mean of the yearly FARs, 0.833228, labelled so it is not taken for the pooled rate. The same 0.826095 is what a rolling window of 5 returns over these years."),

q(3, "The capstone's Tier 1 PSE field names a Tier 1 count. What does working it involve, per the capstone brief?",
 "Rate the stated count on the brief's base.",
 ["Classify each listed release against API RP 754 first, and then rate whichever events come out as Tier 1.",
  "Rate Tier 1 and Tier 2 together, since the capstone asks for all process safety events in one rate.",
  "Use the FAR base, since process safety events are rare in the way fatalities are and share that base."],
 "The brief says the Tier 1 PSE rate takes the Tier 1 count it states; you are not asked to classify anything, because the engine never does. Its worked twin is UGHELLI's Tier 1 rate, 0.086257 per 200,000 hours. The engine rates one tier per call and refuses the FAR base for a PSE rate."),

q(1, "The capstone field for a workforce in parts is a recordable rate. Which twin and which rule does the brief attach to it?",
 "KWALE's pooled 0.968312 and sum then divide.",
 ["KWALE's mean of site rates, 1.754760, and an equal weight for every part of the workforce.",
  "AKASO's first window, 1.208038, and a rolling window over the parts of the workforce listed in the brief.",
  "UGHELLI's 0.776317 and the named base, since a workforce in parts is rated one part at a time."],
 "The brief pairs this field with KWALE pooled, 0.968312, and the rule sum then divide: add every part's recordables, add every part's hours, divide once. The mean of the parts' rates is a different number and is not what is asked for. AKASO is the rolling field's twin and UGHELLI's rate is a single site's."),

q(1, "A contractor reports per head: 3 recordables among 40 people. Your group rates on hours. What must you ask for before the figure can go into your table?",
 "The hours those 40 people actually worked in the period.",
 ["Nothing, since 3 among 40 converts to a rate on the OSHA base by the 100 full-time worker reading of the label.",
  "The headcount of your own group, so the two per head figures can be set on a common base in the engine.",
  "The contractor's own base."],
 "The engine has no headcount input; a headcount says nothing about how long anyone worked. The two crews of 40 read the same 2.500000 per 100 people and 2.500000 and 1.717033 per 200,000 hours because their hours differed. Only the contractor's hours can turn 3 recordables into a rate."),

